{
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "resolution_hours",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.97,
      "source_groundedness": 0.95,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "This is the core dependent variable the query asks about; resolution duration is exactly what must vary across category and priority. Derivable from opened_at and closed_at, which are present. Values are plausible (24.6h, 47.9h, etc.) and consistent with typical incident spans."
    },
    {
      "name": "duration_band",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.85,
      "source_groundedness": 0.93,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "A short/medium/long categorization of resolution_hours gives a clean grouping axis useful for cross-tabbing against category and priority. Somewhat redundant with resolution_hours but adds a usable discretization. Bands match the underlying hours (47.9h and 56.7h -> long, 24.6h -> medium)."
    },
    {
      "name": "duration_vs_category_median",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.9,
      "source_groundedness": 0.9,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Deviation of each incident's duration from its category median directly supports the 'across category' half of the query, normalizing for category baseline. Derived from resolution_hours grouped by the present category column; sample values track resolution_hours sensibly."
    },
    {
      "name": "duration_vs_priority_median",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.9,
      "source_groundedness": 0.9,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Deviation from priority median directly supports the 'across priority' half of the query. Grounded in resolution_hours and the present priority column; values are consistent with the hours in the samples."
    },
    {
      "name": "duration_z_score",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.6,
      "source_groundedness": 0.9,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "A global standardization of duration is a mild new axis for outlier detection, but it is largely a monotone rescaling of resolution_hours and does not condition on category or priority, so it adds less leverage for THIS grouped query than the two median-deviation columns. Values are correctly derived from resolution_hours."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.87,
  "source_groundedness": 0.92,
  "rationale": "The added columns are well targeted to the query: resolution_hours is the key measure, duration_band gives a grouping axis, and the two median-deviation columns are almost purpose-built for comparing duration across category and priority. All values are soundly derived from present fields (opened_at/closed_at, category, priority). The z-score is somewhat redundant with resolution_hours and does not condition on the grouping dimensions, slightly lowering the holistic usefulness, but it remains grounded and no hallucinations were observed."
}

